All three dataflows land within 1% of each other: 13,107 cycles for weight-stationary, 13,167 for output-stationary and 13,231 for input-stationary. That points to a DRAM bandwidth floor. The three operands total about 84k elements (16k input, 64k filter, 4k output), and moving them takes roughly 13k cycles at about 6.4 elements per cycle, whatever the dataflow.

Weight-stationary still had the lowest total and the most non-stall cycles (about 5,055). That leaves the most room for a larger array to help. A 64×64 array uses the full 4,096-PE budget. It halves the weight-stationary folds from 32 to 16 (512/64 × 128/64) and may let compute overlap more with memory traffic. If the cycle count doesn't move, the bandwidth floor is confirmed.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary cut the run from 57,059 to 19,219 cycles, and stalls fell to 5,012. Compute is now the larger share, at about 14.2k cycles. With ws on a 32×32 array, K=128 and N=512 split into 4×16 = 64 folds, and each fold streams only M=128 rows, so fill and drain take a large fraction of every fold.

Input-stationary (is) holds input tiles in the array (K×M = 4×4 = 16 folds) and streams the 512 filter columns through each one. Each fold is longer, so fill and drain should cost less per useful cycle. I'll keep the 32×32 shape so this compares only the dataflow.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The first run took 13,119 cycles, and 11,956 of them were stalls. Compute alone was about 1,160 cycles, close to my estimate. That means the design is limited by memory bandwidth, not by compute.

The weight matrix (512×128, about 64K elements) makes up most of the data that has to be moved. Weight-stationary loads each weight into the array once and keeps it there, which could cut stalls even though it needs more compute cycles. I'll test weight-stationary on a 64×64 array, which uses the full 4096-PE budget.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Both designs land at about 13,100 cycles even though their compute and stall splits differ a lot. Output-stationary had about 1,163 compute cycles and 11,956 stalls. Weight-stationary had about 3,167 compute cycles and 9,940 stalls. Total time seems to be set by how fast the 512×128 weight matrix can be brought in, at roughly 5 elements per cycle. The dataflow mostly changes how much compute overlaps with that transfer.

Input-stationary holds the small 8×512 input in the array and streams each weight through exactly once. That may overlap compute with weight transfer better than the other two. I'll test it on 64×64 to see whether it gets below the roughly 13.1K-cycle floor.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The three runs land within 1% of each other: 13,107 cycles for weight-stationary, 13,119 for output-stationary and 13,231 for input-stationary. That supports the idea that the limit is the time to bring in the 512×128 weights from memory, not the dataflow. Weight-stationary at 64×64 is the best result so far.

A smaller weight-stationary tile may help a little. A 32×64 array loads weights in more, smaller pieces, which gives the array more chances to compute while the next weights arrive. Its compute time will be longer, but that may not matter if most of it overlaps with the weight transfer. I'll test 32×64 next.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary on 32×64 also took 13,107 cycles, exactly the same as on 64×64. Stalls dropped from 9,940 to 8,820, but the extra compute cycles made up the difference. All four runs have landed between 13,107 and 13,231 cycles. That points to a fixed limit: moving the roughly 70 KB of data (mostly the 512×128 weights) at about 5.4 bytes per cycle takes about 13.1K cycles. Changing the array shape or dataflow only changes how compute overlaps with that transfer, so another simulation is unlikely to beat 13,107.

I'm submitting weight-stationary on 32×64. It ties for the lowest cycle count and uses half as many PEs as the 64×64 design.

{"answer": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The input-stationary 32×32 run is memory-bound. It took 13,167 cycles, and 10,744 of them were stalls. That leaves about 2,420 compute cycles, which matches my estimate, so the mapping works and the memory traffic is the problem.

Next I'll test output-stationary (os) on 32×32. With os, each 32×32 output tile stays in its PE until it is finished, so no partial sums move between folds. The small input matrix (32×128) stays in SRAM and gets reused across all 16 folds. Each column slice of the filter is streamed once. That traffic pattern may stall less, even though os needs more compute cycles.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary cut the run to 65,335 cycles, and only 9,016 of those were stalls. The rest is compute plus pipeline overhead. The 16×16 array needs 16 × 32 = 512 folds (K/16 × N/16). Each fold streams only 49 input pixels but also spends about 32 cycles filling and draining the array, so overhead roughly matches useful work.

Output-stationary should cut that overhead. It holds outputs in place and streams K = 256 through each fold, so the fill and drain cost gets spread over a much longer stream. On an 8×32 array, M = 49 maps onto 8 rows (7 folds, 49/56 use) and N = 512 onto 32 columns (16 folds). That gives 112 folds of about 256 + 40 cycles, roughly 33k cycles before stalls. Each filter tile is 32 × 256 elements, about 8 kB, so it fits in the filter SRAM. The inputs are also small, so stalls should stay low if the simulator orders the loops to reuse data.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 8, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary was the worst run so far: 610,463 cycles, of which 572,832 were stalls. The compute part, about 37.6k cycles, matched my estimate. The stalls mean the simulator does not keep the filter tiles resident across the 7 output-pixel folds. It reloads the full 128 kB of filters from memory each time, the same failure that hurt input-stationary. Weight-stationary is the only dataflow that keeps stalls low here, so I'll use the remaining calls to pick its array shape.

Under weight-stationary, K maps onto the rows and N onto the columns. Both 16×16 and 8×32 need 512 folds. In each fold the array loads its weights, streams the 49 input pixels, and drains. On 16×16 that overhead is about 16 + 16 cycles. On 8×32 the weight load drops to 8 rows, but the drain gets longer across 32 columns. The 8×32 tiles also change the memory access pattern, which could cut the 9k stall cycles. The 32×8 shape would make the weight load longer, and 8×8 needs 4× the folds, so I'm ruling both out. Next I'll test 8×32.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 8, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 8×32 weight-stationary run returned exactly the same result as 16×16: 65,335 cycles and 9,016 stall cycles. Both shapes have 256 PEs and need 512 folds, so in this simulator the cycle count seems to depend on the fold count and total PE count, not on how the rows and columns are split. Smaller shapes such as 8×16 or 8×8 can only add folds, so I've ruled them out.

The only full-budget weight-stationary shape I haven't tested is 32×8. It is the mirror of 8×32, so I expect the same result. Still, input-stationary behaved differently on 32×8, and I have calls to spare. I'll run it before submitting so I don't miss an asymmetric effect.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 32×8 weight-stationary run took 68,607 cycles with no stalls. That makes it 3,272 cycles slower than 16×16 and 8×32, which both took 65,335 cycles including 9,016 stall cycles. So the row/column split does matter, and my last guess that only fold count and PE count count was wrong. With 32 rows, each fold has a longer fill and drain. That adds about 12.3k cycles of compute (68,607 against 56,319), which is more than the 9k stall cycles it removes.

At 256 PEs, the only full-budget shapes allowed are 8×32, 16×16 and 32×8, and I have tested all three with weight-stationary. Any smaller array needs more folds, and both other dataflows stalled for hundreds of thousands of cycles. I'm keeping the last call unused and submitting 16×16 weight-stationary. It tied 8×32 for the lowest cycle count.

{"answer": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

I'll start with the configuration I expect to be fastest. The array is 32×32, which uses all 1024 PEs, with output-stationary (os) dataflow.

My estimate: os splits the 128×128 output into 16 tiles of 32×32. Each tile takes about 512 + 32 + 32 = 576 cycles, so the total is about 9.2k cycles. With weight-stationary (ws), I estimate 64 tiles of about 192 cycles each, roughly 12.3k cycles. The inner dimension of 512 is long, so os should win because it streams that dimension without reloading weights.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 32×32 os run took 54,743 cycles, and 45,560 of those (83%) were stalls. That leaves about 9.2k compute cycles, which matches my estimate. Memory traffic is the bottleneck, not compute.

The likely cause is SRAM size. Each operand has 65,536 elements, so at more than one byte per element it won't fit in its 64 kB buffer, and os reloads operands for every output tile. Weight-stationary (ws) loads each weight block once and reuses it across all 128 input rows, so it may move less data even though it does more compute passes. I'll test ws at the same 32×32 shape so the dataflow is the only change.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 16×64 ws run took 28,040 cycles, the best so far. That is 47% below 32×32 ws, and stalls dropped from 38,240 to 13,833. My model held: each time input re-reads halve, stalls fall by 2.3–2.8×. Width 64 is the largest allowed, so ws can't push re-reads below 2.

Input-stationary (is) is the mirror image of ws. It holds an input block in place and re-streams the filter. Its re-reads scale with M / array_w, which is also 128 / 64 = 2, because M = N = 128 and both operands have 65,536 elements. Total traffic should be close to ws. The simulator's operand layout and bandwidth sharing may still treat the two differently, so I'll test is at the same 16×64 shape.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 16×64 is run matched ws exactly: 28,040 cycles and 13,833 stall cycles. With M = N = 128, the two dataflows are mirror images, so the simulator moves the same amount of data for both. That leaves 16×64 ws as the best design so far.

Output-stationary (os) is the one dataflow I haven't tried at a non-square shape. At 16×64 it keeps 16 output tiles. Inputs would be re-read 2 times and filters 8 times, compared with 4 and 4 at 32×32. That adds up to slightly more traffic, but the switch from 32×32 to 16×64 under ws cut stalls by more than the traffic count predicted. I'll use the last call on os at 16×64. Submitting the final answer doesn't need a simulator call, so if this run is slower I can still submit 16×64 ws.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary was slower overall: 14,429 cycles versus 13,167 for output-stationary. It did cut stalls from 10,872 to 6,366. Compute grew to about 8,060 cycles, though, because M=32 is small. Each of the 64 weight tiles (16 K-folds × 4 N-folds) pays its fill and drain overhead while streaming only 32 rows.

Input-stationary should do better on this shape. It pins the small input matrix (32×512, 16k elements) in the array: K goes on the 32 rows (16 folds) and M=32 fits exactly in the 32 columns. Each fold then streams N=128 filter columns. That means fewer folds and longer streams than weight-stationary, and each filter element passes through the array once. Next I'll test input-stationary on the same 32×32 array.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary and input-stationary both took exactly 13,167 cycles at 15.55% utilization, even though their compute and stall splits differ (about 2,295 + 10,872 for OS versus 3,551 + 9,616 for IS). Identical totals suggest the run is limited by memory bandwidth: total DRAM traffic sets the cycle count, and the dataflow mostly changes how much compute overlaps with it. Weight-stationary was the only design that went over that floor, because its 64 short weight tiles made compute longer than the transfer time.

To test that, I'll keep output-stationary but change the array shape to 16×64. M=32 then takes 2 row folds and N=128 takes 2 column folds, which is still 4 folds. Each filter tile is 64 columns wide, so the 512×128 filter is read in larger contiguous chunks. If the cycle count stays near 13,167, the design is bandwidth-bound and shape has little effect. If it drops, filter re-fetch is the cost and wider arrays help.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 16x16 output-stationary run finished in 2,527 cycles with no stalls, close to my estimate of about 2,560. The other output-stationary shapes that fit 256 PEs, 32x8 and 8x32, should be slightly worse at roughly 168 cycles per tile instead of 160.

Input-stationary on 16x16 should come close. It holds 16 tiles of the 128x32 input in the array, and the 128 output columns stream through each one in about 160 cycles. That puts it near output-stationary, so it is worth one call to compare.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The input-stationary run took 2,783 cycles, which is worse than output-stationary's 2,527. So output-stationary on 16x16 is still the best result.

My model for output-stationary fits the first result closely. Each tile costs about K + h + w − 2 cycles, which is 158 cycles on 16x16, and 16 tiles × 158 = 2,528 cycles. Under the same model, a 32x8 array maps all 32 rows of M at once and needs 16 column passes. Each pass costs about 166 cycles, so it should come out slightly worse, around 2,656 cycles. I'll run it to check whether the simulator's fill and drain costs match the model when the array isn't square.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The IS run took 13,231 cycles. Only about 1,403 of those were compute, which matches my estimate. The other 11,828 were stall cycles, and utilization was 3.9%. So the limit is memory bandwidth: the array waits for data most of the time.

Next I'll test output-stationary (OS) on the same 64×64 array. OS keeps each partial sum in its PE until it is finished, so outputs are written once instead of once per K fold. It also reads the filter only once, and the input matrix it re-reads is small (32×128). That should move less data than IS.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary (OS) also took exactly 13,231 cycles, the same as input-stationary (IS). Its stall count was lower (11,200 vs 11,828), so its compute time was longer (about 2,031 cycles vs about 1,403). The two totals matching exactly suggests a fixed floor set by memory bandwidth. The likely cause is the data that must be moved at least once: the 128×512 filter is 64K elements, much more than the 4K-element input or the 16K-element output. If so, the dataflow only decides how much of that transfer time compute hides.

Next I'll test weight-stationary (WS) on the 64×64 array. WS reads each filter element exactly once, and the filter is the largest matrix. That makes WS the dataflow most likely to get below this floor. If WS also returns 13,231, the floor holds for every dataflow on this array.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 32×32 weight-stationary (WS) run took 14,429 cycles, 1,576 more than 64×32 WS. Stalls dropped only a little, from 6,774 to 6,366. Compute rose from about 6,080 to about 8,060 cycles. Halving the array height doubled the number of K folds, and the small saving in stalls did not make up for that. That leaves 64×32 WS as the best result so far, at 12,853 cycles.

The runs so far suggest that a narrower array lowers stalls, while a taller array cuts compute. For the last simulator call I'll keep the 64×32 shape and switch to input-stationary (IS). With IS, the 32 array columns hold exactly M = 32, so no columns go unused. K = 128 takes 2 row folds, and N = 512 streams through each fold. That gives a compute estimate of about 2 × (128 + 32 + 512) ≈ 1,340 cycles, much less than WS on the same array. If its stalls come in close to 64×32 WS, IS should beat 12,853. If not, I'll submit 64×32 WS.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 16x64 OS run took 823 cycles, which matches the predicted 4 × 206 = 824. So the simulator maps M (32) to the array rows and N (128) to the columns, and the per-fold cost is about K + h + w − 2. Under that model, 32x32 is the best OS shape within the 1024-PE budget. Every other shape either needs more folds or has a longer fill-and-drain time.

Weight-stationary has to cycle through 16 weight tiles of 32x32, so it should be much slower. Input-stationary with the 32x128 input pinned on a 32x32 array also needs 4 folds, while streaming N = 128. It could tie with OS or come in slightly under it, so I'll test it next.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary on 16×16 brought the total down to 110,444 cycles, but 53k of those are still stall cycles. Each filter is fetched only once, so the remaining stalls probably come from the 10 input-channel folds (160/16). Each fold produces partial sums that get written to the output buffer and read back, and each of the 600 folds loads a new weight tile.

Output-stationary avoids partial sums because every output is accumulated inside the array and written once. On a 32×8 array, the 32 rows hold output pixels (49 → 2 folds) and the 8 columns hold filters (960 → 120 folds). That gives 240 folds of about 160 + 32 + 8 = 200 cycles each, or roughly 48k compute cycles. Filters are streamed only twice (about 307k elements), compared with 7 times for input-stationary. The inputs (7.8 kB) stay in SRAM, and each output (47 kB) is written once.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The output-stationary run on 64×64 took 52,181 cycles. Stalls account for 46,752 of them and utilization is only 7%, so the design is limited by memory bandwidth rather than compute. The filters are the likely cause. They total 960 × 320 ≈ 307 kB, which does not fit in the 64 kB filter SRAM, so they have to stream from memory. The inputs (47 kB) and outputs (15.7 kB) both fit on-chip.

Weight-stationary loads each filter into the array once and reuses it across all 49 pixels. The inputs and partial sums can stay in SRAM. That may cut stall cycles even though weight-stationary uses more folds, so I'll test it next on the same 64×64 array.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary is much worse: 462,352 cycles, of which 444,428 are stalls. That leaves about 17.9k compute cycles, against about 5.4k for output-stationary. In this simulator, weight-stationary seems to refetch inputs and partial sums across its 75 folds, and that multiplies memory traffic. Output-stationary's compute time matched my estimate, but its runtime is still mostly memory stalls.

Input-stationary is the dataflow I haven't tested. It keeps 64-channel slices of the 49 input pixels in the array and streams each filter element through once. That should put its filter traffic close to output-stationary's, over 15 longer folds, which may hide memory latency better. I'll test it on the same 64×64 array.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Input-stationary took 247,155 cycles, with 239,506 of them stalls. That puts it between the other two, and output-stationary on 64×64 is still the best at 52,181 cycles. In all three runs, stall cycles make up 92–96% of the runtime. So the dataflow that moves the least data between memory and SRAM wins, and that is output-stationary.

Next I'll test whether a narrower array reduces stalls. For output-stationary, the 49 output pixels map onto the 64 rows, and the 320 filters split into column passes (folds). A 64×32 array keeps one row fold and has 10 column folds instead of 5. Compute time roughly doubles, to about 10k cycles, but each filter is still fetched only once. Each fold then needs half as many filters at a time, which lowers the required memory bandwidth per cycle. If stalls come from each fold asking for data faster than memory can deliver, 64×32 should stall less. If they come from total data volume, cycles should stay near 52k.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

My estimate of about 40k cycles was wrong by roughly 3x. The run took 123,881 cycles. About 40.4k of those were compute, which matches my estimate, but 83,434 were stalls, so this layer is memory-bound under os. The math floor is 128 × 128 × 512 = 8.39M MACs, which on 256 PEs is about 32.8k cycles. That means most of the time goes to data movement, not arithmetic.

Weight-stationary (ws) keeps each filter tile in the array and streams the small 128 × 128 input past it. That should cut repeated fetches of the larger 128 × 512 filter. I'll test ws at 16×16 so the dataflow is the only thing that changes.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The input-stationary (is) run took 104,895 cycles, which makes it the wrong choice. Its compute portion was 104,895 − 69,184 = 35,711 cycles, which matches my 35.7k estimate. But it stalled for 69,184 cycles. Streaming all 512 filter columns through every fold seems to exceed the fixed memory bandwidth. I predicted the compute cost but missed the stalls. Weight-stationary (ws) is the only dataflow so far with zero stalls, so I'll tune the array shape under ws.

My ws model is cycles = (128/h) × (512/w) × (2h + w + 126). It fit the 16×16 run to within 1 cycle. Under it, 8×32 also comes out at 256 folds × 174 = 44,544 cycles, a tie with 16×16, and 32×8 comes out worse at 50,688. However, a single data point can't tell me whether the doubled term belongs to h or w. If it belongs to w, then 8×32 would cost 198 cycles per fold and 32×8 would tie instead. Running ws at 8×32 settles which reading is correct. It also checks whether the wider array starts stalling.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 8, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

I'll start with a 64x64 array, which uses the full 4096-PE budget, and the output-stationary (os) dataflow.

For this GEMM, M=128, K=512 and N=128. Output-stationary holds the 128x128 output block as 4 tiles of 64x64. Each tile streams the full K=512, so it takes about 512 + 128 = 640 cycles, or roughly 2,560 cycles for all 4 tiles. Weight-stationary (ws) and input-stationary (is) would need about 16 tiles of shorter length, which works out to roughly 4,000 cycles. That estimate favors output-stationary, so I'll check it with the simulator first.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The 32x32 weight-stationary run took 52,447 cycles, about twice the 64x64 result. Stall cycles also rose, from 21,136 to 38,240. So my bandwidth-per-cycle theory was wrong. Stalls seem to grow with the number of tile passes, which suggests each pass reloads its operands from memory. A smaller array needs more passes, so it stalls more.

Shrinking the array from 64x64 to 32x32 doubled the cycle count. Any shape smaller than 64x64 means at least as many passes, so it should do no better. The best result so far is 26,223 cycles. Weight-stationary and input-stationary both reached it on the 64x64 array, which uses the full 4,096-PE budget. I'll submit 64x64 weight-stationary and keep the last 2 simulator calls unused.

{"answer": {"array_h": 64, "array_w": 64, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Output-stationary on 16x16 ran at 27.8% utilization. Stalls made up 83,216 of the 117,903 cycles, so the design is limited by memory bandwidth rather than compute. The ideal compute time is about 32.8k cycles: 128×512×128 MACs divided by 256 PEs.

The next test is weight-stationary on the same 16x16 array. It keeps each filter tile in the PEs and streams only the inputs, which could reduce the operand traffic that is causing the stalls.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary cut the cycle count to 104,895, which is 11% fewer than output-stationary, and lowered stalls to 60,352. Stalls still account for most of the runtime, so the bottleneck is still memory traffic.

The input matrix (128×512) and filter matrix (512×128) are the same size, so input-stationary could behave much like weight-stationary. Its traffic pattern is different, though, and its stall profile may differ. I'll test input-stationary on the same 16x16 array to finish the dataflow comparison before trying other array shapes.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "is", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Input-stationary gave exactly the same result as weight-stationary: 104,895 cycles and 60,352 stall cycles. That matches the symmetry I expected, since the input and filter matrices are the same size. So the dataflow choice is now between two equal options, and array shape is the variable left to test.

On 16x16 weight-stationary, about 44.5k of the cycles are compute and 60.4k are stalls. A non-square array changes how the 512-long reduction dimension is split into tiles, and that changes how often operands have to be refetched. I'll test a tall 32x8 array with weight-stationary. It puts 32 rows along K, so the 512-long reduction needs half as many passes as on 16x16.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 32, "array_w": 8, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The tall 32x8 weight-stationary array was about twice as slow: 209,791 cycles against 104,895 on 16x16. Stalls rose to 159,104, so my guess that fewer passes over K would reduce refetches was wrong. Here is the likely cause. With only 8 columns, each input row streamed through the array produces 8 outputs instead of 16, so the same input traffic does half as much useful work. The input stream is the operand limited by memory bandwidth, which makes this configuration worse.

If that explanation is right, the opposite shape should help. A wide 8x32 array makes 32 outputs from each streamed input, which doubles reuse compared with 16x16. It also needs 64 passes over K instead of 32, which adds fill and drain overhead. I'll test 8x32 weight-stationary to see which effect is larger.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 8, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The wide 8x32 weight-stationary array confirmed the reuse explanation. It ran in 44,543 cycles with zero stalls at 73.6% utilization, 58% fewer cycles than 16x16 weight-stationary. Memory bandwidth no longer limits it. The remaining overhead above the ideal 32.8k cycles comes from filling and draining the array on each of its 64 passes over K.

Output-stationary avoids most of that overhead because each output tile accumulates over the full K=512 in one pass. On 8x32 it would run 64 output tiles at about 552 cycles each, roughly 35.3k cycles if it stays stall-free. On 16x16 it was limited by memory bandwidth, but the wider shape gives better input reuse here too. I'll spend the last call on 8x32 output-stationary. If it doesn't beat 44,543 cycles, I'll submit 8x32 weight-stationary.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 8, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "os", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The output-stationary prediction was wrong. On 8x32 it took 218,691 cycles, with 183,492 of them stalls and 14.98% utilization. That is about five times slower than weight-stationary on the same shape. Making the array wider helped weight-stationary, but in output-stationary on this simulator the wide shape apparently creates more operand traffic than memory bandwidth can supply. My reuse argument doesn't carry over between dataflows.

All six simulator calls are used. The best measured design is 8x32 weight-stationary: 44,543 cycles, zero stalls and 73.6% utilization. That is 58% fewer cycles than the next-best result, 104,895 on 16x16 with weight- or input-stationary. I didn't test 8x32 input-stationary. It might match weight-stationary as it did on 16x16, but the input and filter roles reverse, so I'm submitting the measured design.

{"answer": {"array_h": 8, "array_w": 32, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

The two runs finished in exactly the same number of cycles (13,119), and their utilization matched too. That points to a memory-bandwidth floor rather than a dataflow effect. The job moves 1 kB of inputs, 64 kB of weights and 4 kB of outputs, about 70.6 kB in total. At 13,119 cycles that works out to roughly 5.4 bytes per cycle, which is probably the fixed bandwidth. The weights alone fill the 64 kB filter SRAM exactly, so the simulator likely can't double-buffer them and stalls during the load.

Weight-stationary (WS) is the remaining dataflow to test. It fetches weights in small tiles, one array-sized block per fold, so it might overlap fetch with compute better and get under this floor. I'll test it on a full 1024-PE array (32×32). If it also lands at about 13,119 cycles, the bound is confirmed and the choice between designs barely matters.

