The weight-stationary (WS) 32×32 run finished in 13,021 cycles, 98 fewer than the other two. All three designs sit near the same memory floor of about 13.1k cycles. That fits the fixed bandwidth having to move about 64 kB of weights. Of the WS run's 13,021 cycles, 6,494 were stalls, so its remaining stall time largely overlaps compute.

The small win for WS probably comes from output traffic. WS writes partial sums once per fold along K. A 32-row array needs 4 folds over K=128, while a 64-row array needs only 2, which halves the partial-sum writes. I'll test WS at 64×16, which uses the full 1024-PE budget.

