The 16x16 output-stationary design took 27,267 cycles, and 18,596 of them were stalls. Only about 8.7k cycles were compute, which matches my estimate, so memory bandwidth is the bottleneck. In output-stationary mode, the array needs h + w = 32 new operands from memory every cycle.

Next I'll test weight-stationary on the same 16x16 array. Each weight tile is loaded once and reused across all 32 input rows. That should cut the operand traffic per cycle and show whether the dataflow or the array shape matters more for stalls.

{"simulate": {"array_h": 16, "array_w": 16, "dataflow": "ws", "sram_split": "balanced"}}

Weight-stationary removed every stall, so 16x16 ws finishes in 19,967 cycles. Compute dominates now. The ideal is 32 × 512 × 128 / 256 = 8,192 cycles, so the array is 41% utilized. Weight-stationary splits the work into 256 short folds of about 78 cycles each, and fill and drain take up most of each fold.

Input-stationary should cut that overhead. It holds a (K × M) input tile in the array and streams the 128 filter columns through. On 16x16 that means 32 × 2 = 64 folds of about 128 + 48 ≈ 176 cycles, roughly 11.3k cycles in total. It also reads fewer operands per cycle than output-stationary, so it may avoid the stalls that hurt the first design.

