Input-stationary on 16x16 took 26,223 cycles, with 15,088 of them stalls, so weight-stationary at 19,967 cycles is still the best result. Weight-stationary has little room to improve by reshaping. Its fold count times fold length (about 2h + w + 30 cycles) gives the same total for 8x32, and a worse total for 32x8 or for smaller arrays.

Output-stationary had the least compute, about 8.7k cycles, so it is worth one more look. On 16x16, the 32 output rows split into two row folds, which likely means the full 512x128 filter is streamed from memory twice. A 32x8 array covers all 32 rows in one row fold. That should stream the filter only once and keep the 32x512 input in its SRAM. This would roughly halve memory traffic and cut most of the stalls, while compute stays near 16 × 550 ≈ 8.8k cycles.

