OS was much worse than I expected: 32,879 cycles, of which 28,590 were stalls. Compute matched the ~4.3k estimate, so stalls account for almost all of the loss. Total DRAM traffic doesn't explain the result. The likely cause is how much data the array consumes each cycle. With M = 49 rows and 64 columns, OS has to read about 113 new operands per cycle: 49 inputs plus 64 weights. IS streams only about 64 weights in and 49 outputs out per cycle. Because memory bandwidth is fixed, the dataflow that reads the most operands per cycle stalls the most.

WS (weight stationary) is the dataflow I haven't tested. It keeps a 64×64 tile of weights in place, streams the 49 pixels through, and reloads weights only between folds. The weights are read from DRAM once, and the input reuse is good. It has more folds (45), but its per-cycle input demand is under 64 operands, so stalls may be lower. I'll measure it before tuning the array shape.

