The 64x16 output-stationary run cut cycles to 210,161, a 32% drop from 307,421 but short of the 50% I estimated. Stalls are still 90% of the total (189,402 cycles), so the layer remains memory-bound. Moving the weights once is now the main cost.

The input-stationary dataflow hasn't been tried yet. In it, the array width maps to output pixels and the array height maps to input channels. A 16x64 input-stationary array places all 49 pixels in one column fold. Each input tile (16 channels × 49 pixels) loads once, and each filter value streams through the array once. Weight traffic stays at one pass, as in the 64x16 output-stationary run. The 49 × 320 partial-sum array is about 15.7k values, so it may fit in the 64 kB output SRAM. If it does, the 60 channel folds can accumulate on chip without the spill traffic that made weight-stationary slow.

