The input-stationary 16x64 run took 31,017 cycles, a 6.8x drop from the best output-stationary run. Stalls fell to 6,178 cycles and utilization rose to 47%, so the layer is now mostly compute-bound.

In this layout, the 49 pixels fill 49 of the 64 columns (77%). The layer also needs 60 channel folds, and each one streams 320 filter values plus about 80 cycles of pipeline fill. A 32x32 input-stationary array keeps the same 60 total folds (2 pixel folds × 30 channel folds), with the same 77% column usage across them. Its fill per fold is shorter, about 64 cycles instead of 80. It also halves the number of channel folds that read and update the output partial sums. That should cut some of the remaining stall cycles.

