At width 8, compute cost about 142 cycles per fold across 128 folds, roughly 18.2k cycles in total. A 64×16 array would have 64 folds (2 K-folds × 32 column folds). At a similar cost per fold, that is about 9–10k cycles of compute. This is still below the ~12.9k memory floor, so the shorter pipeline tail might save a few more cycles. The risk is that the extra folds overlap poorly with memory transfers and push the total above 12,901. I'll use my last simulator call on this design. Next turn I'll submit whichever of 64×16 and 64×32 is faster.

