Weight-stationary on 32x8 took 19,967 cycles with zero stalls, so it is compute-bound. With K=128 on 32 rows (4 folds) and N=512 on 8 columns (64 folds), it runs 256 folds that each stream only M=8 rows. That works out to 19,967 / 256 ≈ 78 cycles per fold, which fits about 2h + w + M − 2. The fixed cost of filling and draining each fold outweighs the useful work.

Input-stationary and output-stationary both stopped at exactly 13,119 cycles even though their compute differed (2,327 vs 2,655 non-stall cycles). That points to a memory-transfer floor near 13,119 cycles for those two dataflows. Weight-stationary avoided stalls, so it is the only dataflow that might beat that floor if a different shape cuts its compute. On 16x16, there would be 8 K-folds × 32 N-folds = 256 folds. My per-fold formula predicts about 54 cycles each (≈13,800 total), but the formula comes from a single data point. This run checks it.

